Cause: the sweep wrote its record after the call and the check, and its time limit was set inside the job's.
Fix, done: the deposit is booked once with its own time (wallet.book_deposit, the script's own procedure for a deposit that landed; sweep.jsonl notes it), and wallet.py writes a deposit as `sent` before its call; the next pass settles a `sent` row from the treasury (booked with the time it was sent, or kept unsure) and a deposit starts only in the first 90 s of a pass (commit 92417be2; three tests fail on the old code). Its first live pass at 21:52 UTC wrote sent, then booked 63,721 cr. After the booking the last hour reads +47,231 cr steady.
Would it have prevented this one: yes, the `sent` row is on disk before the call; the gifts of the sweep still book after their check and stay open to the same cut (DECISIONS Q75).

