Cause: (1) a retiring worker stops taking runs before the supervisor has accepted the handover, and nothing hands acceptance back when the supervisor refuses; (2) one install that copies several files the host runs starts one handover per file as each lands, so one install used two of the three slots.
Fix: rescue by hand at 03:01 UTC: stop_pilot.py on the three runs of the oldest retiring worker (10723); it ended, and worker 21077 took runs from 03:02:10 UTC. Structural, proposed to Trinity, who built the handover (queue.log this turn): a worker keeps accepting until the supervisor confirms its replacement, and a change seen is acted on only after the files have stood still for some seconds, so one install is one handover. Mine meanwhile: one install per turn for the files the host runs. Would it have prevented the original: (1) yes, the host would have kept taking runs while the cap held.

